rs-helicopter-succeeds-in-historic-first-flight/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Historic first flight, April 19, 2021"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/blog/what-were-learning-about-ingenuitys-flight-control-and-aerodynamic-performance/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Flight control and aerodynamic performance"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button"},"Collect evidence: Instructions travel. Reflexes stay onboard."]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"false"},["SUMMARY",{},"The ground becomes a reference",["SMALL",{},"Open record"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"Visual navigation · observations and estimation"],["P",{},"A downward-facing navigation camera tracked visible surface features between images. Together with other sensors, those observations helped estimate motion. Distinct features on relatively flat ground are easier to follow than smooth, repetitive sand."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"Reading the illustrative observations"],["P",{},"A cluster of separated pebbles gives recognizable points that can be compared between frames. A blank patch offers few points. Steep, repeating ripples also challenge assumptions about the ground. Texture alone does not prove that terrain is safe to land on."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","The three observation drawings are invented teaching diagrams, not NASA flight images. No precise camera lens is identified by a hotspot in this model."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/press_kits/mars_2020/ingenuity/landing/mission/spacecraft/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA/JPL · Spacecraft engineering"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.