{"type":"frame-snapshot","snapshot":{"callId":"kluo@2635","phase":"after","pageId":"page@5b15a22c69d473627c07483f4a11bce9","frameId":"frame@31a70835f1bfcaaedefde6d414c31ef3","frameUrl":"http://127.0.0.1:4173/","doctype":"html","html":["HTML",{"__playwright_scroll_top_":"2013","lang":"en"},[[398,10]],[[412,19]],["BODY",{},[[412,20]],["DIV",{"id":"root"},["DIV",{"class":"app-shell","data-reduced-motion":"true"},[[412,22]],[[404,5]],["MAIN",{"id":"main","class":"main-content "},["SECTION",{"class":"interior-screen"},[[398,12]],[[409,10]],[[401,10]],["DIV",{"class":"inspection","data-mission":"ingenuity"},[[396,1]],["SECTION",{"class":"quest-header","aria-label":"Ingenuity investigation"},["DIV",{"class":"quest-heading-row"},["DIV",{},[[87,1]],["H2",{"tabindex":"-1"},"Investigation complete"]],[[387,10]]],["OL",{"class":"quest-progress","aria-label":"Mission progress","style":"--quest-count: 6;"},[[330,3]],[[281,3]],[[210,3]],[[153,3]],[[90,3]],["LI",{"data-state":"completed"},[[387,48]],["SPAN",{},[[387,49]],["SMALL",{},"completed"]]]],[[387,123]]],[[396,20]],["DIV",{"class":"inspection-layout"},["DIV",{"class":"inspection-stage"},["DIV",{"class":"spacecraft-viewport","data-model-state":"loaded","data-camera-mode":"rotors","data-model-meshes":"35","data-model-materials":"10","data-model-textures":"8","data-model-triangles":"9124","data-camera-position":"4.852,3.326,4.882"},[[77,4]],["DIV",{"style":"position: relative; width: 100%; height: 100%; overflow: hidden; pointer-events: auto; touch-action: none;"},["DIV",{"style":"width: 100%; height: 100%;"},["CANVAS",{"__playwright_bounding_rect__":"{\"left\":61,\"top\":-1219.09375,\"right\":1079,\"bottom\":-549.09375}","style":"display: block; width: 1018px; height: 670px; touch-action: auto;","data-engine":"three.js r180","width":"1018","height":"670"},[[394,2]]]]]],[[396,29]]],[[78,3]]],["SECTION",{"id":"quest-workbench","class":"mission-conclusion","aria-label":"Ingenuity mission conclusion"},["P",{"class":"eyebrow"},"6"," INVESTIGATIONS / ONE RECOVERED STORY"],["H2",{},"Seventy-two flights. A new way to explore."],["P",{"class":"conclusion-lead"},"Five were the plan. The rest changed what engineers could ask for next."],["P",{},"The first question was whether a small helicopter could fly on Mars at all. You followed the air, the energy and the decisions that made the answer possible."],["P",{},"Ingenuity went on to offer another view of the ground: one that could help a rover team decide where to look and how to get there. The experiment had become useful in ways a short first hop could only suggest."],["P",{},"The final flight left damaged blades and an incomplete view of the landing. Engineers built the best explanation the evidence allowed, and kept the uncertainty visible. That is part of the work, too."],["P",{},"Ingenuity will not take off again. The next aircraft can still learn from every flight it made."],["OL",{"class":"conclusion-findings"},["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"The Five-Flight Gamble"],["P",{},"A short flight could answer the original question. NASA first needed proof that controlled flight on Mars could work."]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"Flight in Thin Air"],["P",{},"Mars offered less weight to lift, but much less air to work with. The aircraft’s light body and rotor design addressed that mismatch together."]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"Surviving the Martian Night"],["P",{},"Daylight helped pay for the night. A safe landing mattered, but survival depended on charge, insulation and heat as well."]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"No Pilot at the Controls"],["P",{},"The people chose the plan. Ingenuity had to make the rapid corrections. Its view of the ground was part of how it knew it was moving."]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"Beyond Five Flights"],["P",{},"The experiment grew into an aerial scout and a longer engineering test. Seventy-two flights meant many chances to learn beyond the original five."]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"The Seventy-Second Flight"],["P",{},"The evidence establishes damaged blades. The investigation offers a most-likely chain from weak visual references to a hard landing. Both the explanation and its limits can guide the next design."]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"completed-findings"},["SUMMARY",{},"Review the ","13"," recovered evidence records"],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-grid"},["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"An experiment with five flights",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"Before the first flight · technology demonstration"],["P",{},"Ingenuity was sent to test powered, controlled flight on Mars. Up to five experimental flights were planned in a 30-day demonstration window. It was not a miniature version of Perseverance’s science laboratory."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"What counted as a useful result?"],["P",{},"Engineers needed evidence that a very light aircraft could take off, stay under control and land in the Martian atmosphere. Even a short flight would test a capability that had never been demonstrated on another planet."]],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"Equipment and purpose"],["P",{},"The helicopter carried cameras, computers and sensors for flight and engineering observations, rather than a dedicated scientific instrument payload. Its small housing had to fit those systems alongside batteries and heaters."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","Judge the original mission against what it was designed to prove, rather than assuming it had a rover’s job."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/press_kits/ingenuity/landing/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA/JPL · Original demonstration objectives"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/press_kits/mars_2020/ingenuity/landing/mission/spacecraft/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA/JPL · Spacecraft engineering"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: An experiment with five flights"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"From a rover’s belly to open sky",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"February–April 2021 · arrival and deployment"],["P",{},"Ingenuity arrived at Jezero Crater beneath Perseverance on February 18, 2021. It was deployed onto the surface on April 3. After surviving on its own, it first flew on April 19."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"The first flight"],["P",{},"The autonomous test rose to about 3 metres, hovered, then landed. The flight lasted 39.1 seconds. The short journey answered a large engineering question: controlled powered flight on another planet was possible."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","Arrival, deployment and flight were separate achievements. A helicopter still attached to a rover had not yet proved it could fly."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-2020-perseverance/ingenuity-mars-helicopter/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Ingenuity mission and timeline"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasas-ingenuity-mars-helicopter-succeeds-in-historic-first-flight/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Historic first flight, April 19, 2021"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/solar-system/nasas-mars-helicopter-survives-first-cold-martian-night-on-its-own/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Surviving the first night alone"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: From a rover’s belly to open sky"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"Less air, less lift",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"Flight environment · qualitative engineering explanation"],["P",{},"Mars has roughly one percent of Earth’s atmospheric density near the surface. Rotors have much less air to push against. Lower gravity reduces the aircraft’s weight, but does not remove the need to generate lift."],["FIGURE",{"class":"science-diagram"},["svg",{"viewBox":"0 0 360 145","role":"img","aria-label":"Illustrative lift diagram: two stacked rotors turn in opposite directions; lift acts upward and weight downward."},["path",{"d":"M180 40v65M60 60h240M85 82h190","stroke":"#bfced0","stroke-width":"4"}],["path",{"d":"M180 32V8l-6 8m6-8 6 8M180 111v25l-6-8m6 8 6-8M70 43q100-35 220 0l-8-13m8 13-16 2M85 98q95 28 190 0l-7 13m7-13-16-2","stroke":"#eccca0","fill":"none","stroke-width":"2"}],["text",{"x":"196","y":"19"},"Lift"],["text",{"x":"196","y":"135"},"Weight"],["text",{"x":"8","y":"128"},"Opposite rotation"]],["FIGCAPTION",{},"Illustrative engineering diagram. Not to scale; no flight telemetry or numerical simulation."]],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"A linked design problem"],["P",{},"Large, rapidly turning blades and a lightweight vehicle help address the thin atmosphere. These choices work together. Lower gravity helps; it does not make a heavy vehicle with small, slow blades a safe substitute."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","The diagram shows relationships, not a calculator for real flight performance. No numerical flight physics is simulated."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/press_kits/ingenuity/landing/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA/JPL · Original demonstration objectives"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: Less air, less lift"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"Two rotors, opposite directions",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"Rotor system · lift and control"],["P",{},"Two stacked rotors turn in opposite directions. Their opposing torques help prevent the body from simply spinning the other way. The blades are carbon-fibre structures, not solid heavy metal paddles."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"Control is more than speed"],["P",{},"Ingenuity controls flight by adjusting blade pitch: the angle at which blades meet the air. That changes lift and allows control of the aircraft. It is misleading to treat it as a consumer drone controlled only by changing motor speeds."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","Lift, torque and control solve different parts of the flight problem. The static model shows the engineering arrangement, not a running rotor simulation."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/press_kits/mars_2020/ingenuity/landing/mission/spacecraft/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA/JPL · Spacecraft engineering"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/blog/what-were-learning-about-ingenuitys-flight-control-and-aerodynamic-performance/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Flight control and aerodynamic performance"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: Two rotors, opposite directions"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"Daylight has another job",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"Power system · energy must last beyond a flight"],["P",{},"The solar array above the rotors charges the lithium-ion battery system. Stored energy powers flight, electronics and heaters. Sunlight is a source; the battery stores energy for use when the panel is not producing it."],["FIGURE",{"class":"science-diagram"},["svg",{"viewBox":"0 0 360 145","role":"img","aria-label":"Illustrative energy path: sunlight charges the battery, which supplies heaters, electronics and flight motors."},["rect",{"x":"11","y":"40","width":"75","height":"42"}],["rect",{"x":"126","y":"40","width":"85","height":"42"}],["path",{"d":"M87 61h37m88 0h35M247 22v80m0-80h30m-30 40h30m-30 40h30","fill":"none","stroke":"#e2bd91","stroke-width":"2"}],["text",{"x":"16","y":"65"},"Sunlight"],["text",{"x":"136","y":"65"},"Battery"],["text",{"x":"280","y":"26"},"Heaters"],["text",{"x":"260","y":"66"},"Electronics"],["text",{"x":"280","y":"106"},"Motors"]],["FIGCAPTION",{},"Illustrative engineering diagram. Not to scale; no flight telemetry or numerical simulation."]],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"One supply, several demands"],["P",{},"A flight and a cold night draw on the same stored resource. A useful planning strategy protects essential systems and checks the battery’s condition before committing energy to another flight. This is qualitative reasoning, not a historical power-allocation schedule."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","A successful landing still leaves the helicopter with an energy problem to solve."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/mars-helicopters-solar-array-as-seen-by-perseverances-mastcam-z/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Solar array and stored energy"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: Daylight has another job"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"Landing was not the end of the day",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"April 2021 · surviving without rover-supplied power"],["P",{},"After deployment, Ingenuity had to supply its own heater from its own battery. Insulation and heaters protected temperature-sensitive parts inside. The team checked overnight survival and recharging before flight preparations continued."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"Why the rover moved"],["P",{},"Perseverance moved clear so sunlight could reach the helicopter’s panel. The rover no longer supplied power through the old physical connection. Ingenuity needed to recharge and manage its own heat."]],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"A careful trade-off"],["P",{},"Keeping the interior warmer consumes energy. Turning off essential protection to maximise flying time is not a sound survival strategy. Flight preparation also depends on temperatures and available charge."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","“It landed” and “it can survive until tomorrow” are different conclusions. Neither should be assumed from the other."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/solar-system/nasas-mars-helicopter-survives-first-cold-martian-night-on-its-own/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Surviving the first night alone"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/blog/its-cold-on-mars/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · It’s Cold on Mars, April 2, 2021"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: Landing was not the end of the day"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"Instructions travel. Reflexes stay onboard.",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"Flight operations · planning from Earth"],["P",{},"Earth teams planned flights and sent instructions. During flight, onboard software combined sensor information and controlled the helicopter. Communication delays ruled out an Earth operator making immediate joystick corrections."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"A plan is not a remote pilot"],["P",{},"People chose objectives and reviewed results, while the aircraft handled the rapid control decisions needed during flight. This division of work allowed a carefully prepared route to be flown without a live human pilot at Mars."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","The planning challenge here is an educational example. You are not piloting a historical flight or viewing real telemetry."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasas-ingenuity-mars-helicopter-succeeds-in-historic-first-flight/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Historic first flight, April 19, 2021"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/blog/what-were-learning-about-ingenuitys-flight-control-and-aerodynamic-performance/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Flight control and aerodynamic performance"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: Instructions travel. Reflexes stay onboard."]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"The ground becomes a reference",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"Visual navigation · observations and estimation"],["P",{},"A downward-facing navigation camera tracked visible surface features between images. Together with other sensors, those observations helped estimate motion. Distinct features on relatively flat ground are easier to follow than smooth, repetitive sand."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"Reading the illustrative observations"],["P",{},"A cluster of separated pebbles gives recognizable points that can be compared between frames. A blank patch offers few points. Steep, repeating ripples also challenge assumptions about the ground. Texture alone does not prove that terrain is safe to land on."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","The three observation drawings are invented teaching diagrams, not NASA flight images. No precise camera lens is identified by a hotspot in this model."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/press_kits/mars_2020/ingenuity/landing/mission/spacecraft/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA/JPL · Spacecraft engineering"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/missions/mars-2020-perseverance/ingenuity-helicopter/nasa-performs-first-aircraft-accident-investigation-on-another-world/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Accident investigation, December 11, 2024"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: The ground becomes a reference"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"A new job after five",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"After the demonstration · operations and scouting"],["P",{},"After the initial five flights, the mission moved into an operations demonstration. Ingenuity explored how an aerial viewpoint could help rover planning, including observations of terrain that was hard to assess from ground level."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"What changed?"],["P",{},"The technology demonstration asked whether flight worked. Continued operations asked how flight could contribute to exploration. Aerial images could inform choices about routes and interesting areas, while further flights supplied engineering experience."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","A broader role did not turn Ingenuity into a rover, a sample collector or a laboratory with new science instruments."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/mars/nasas-ingenuity-mars-helicopter-reaches-a-total-of-30-minutes-aloft/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · From test flights to aerial scouting"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-legacy-of-ingenuity-the-frontier-of-extraterrestrial-flight/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · The legacy of Ingenuity"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: A new job after five"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"The plan and the record",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"Mission record · completed flights, not promised ones"],["P",{},"The original demonstration planned up to five flights. Ingenuity completed 72 across nearly three years. NASA records more than two hours of total flight time and a maximum altitude of about 24 metres."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"Useful milestones"],["P",{},"The first flight was April 19, 2021. The fifth flight on May 7 completed the initial flight series. NASA marks the fiftieth flight on April 13, 2023. The last was January 18, 2024. These are flight-history milestones, not stages of an animated simulation."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","Longevity matters because repeated operations test hardware, procedures and assumptions over time—not because a larger number automatically answers every scientific question."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/ingenuity-mars-helicopter-by-the-numbers/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Ingenuity by the numbers"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-2020-perseverance/ingenuity-mars-helicopter/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Ingenuity mission and timeline"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/ingenuity-helicopter-flight-log.pdf","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Completed flight log (PDF)"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: The plan and the record"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"A short hop to check the aircraft",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"January 18, 2024 · Flight 72"],["P",{},"Flight 72 was intended as a brief vertical flight to check systems and photograph the area after the preceding flight ended early. Ingenuity climbed to its planned height and began descending. Communications were lost near the end of the flight."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"Read a status report in its time"],["P",{},"The January 19 report described what was known immediately afterward. It was not yet the completed accident investigation. Later evidence and analysis changed what engineers could reasonably explain."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","A report written just after an event and a later investigation answer different questions."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/blog/flight-72-status-update/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA/JPL · Flight 72 status, January 19, 2024"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/ingenuity-helicopter-flight-log.pdf","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Completed flight log (PDF)"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/missions/mars-2020-perseverance/ingenuity-helicopter/nasa-performs-first-aircraft-accident-investigation-on-another-world/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Accident investigation, December 11, 2024"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: A short hop to check the aircraft"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"What the pictures established",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"After Flight 72 · observed damage"],["P",{},"Communications were reestablished the following day. Later imagery showed rotor-blade damage that ended Ingenuity’s ability to fly. The images establish damage; they do not provide a complete eyewitness view of touchdown."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"Keep observation separate from explanation"],["P",{},"An image of a damaged blade is direct evidence of its condition. Describing the exact motions and loads that produced it requires a reconstruction using the available flight data and engineering analysis."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","The 3D model remains an intact engineering visualization. It is not a reconstruction of the damaged aircraft after Flight 72."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/after-three-years-on-mars-nasas-ingenuity-helicopter-mission-ends/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · End of flight mission, January 25, 2024"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/missions/mars-2020-perseverance/ingenuity-helicopter/nasa-performs-first-aircraft-accident-investigation-on-another-world/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Accident investigation, December 11, 2024"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: What the pictures established"]]],["DETAILS",{"class":"evidence-card","data-collected":"true"},["SUMMARY",{},"A most-likely explanation",["SMALL",{},"Collected"]],["DIV",{"class":"evidence-body"},["P",{"class":"record-context"},"December 11, 2024 · later accident-investigation account"],["P",{},"NASA’s investigation linked too few trackable surface features to unreliable motion estimates. Its most-likely scenario involved excessive horizontal motion, a hard impact on a sand-ripple slope, rapid changes in attitude and damaging rotor loads."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"The limit of the evidence"],["P",{},"Engineers considered multiple viable scenarios. The available data supported a most-likely account, not certainty about every motion. Testing navigation over a broader range of terrain and improving robustness are sensible lessons from this case."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","State which parts are observed and which are inferred. An honest uncertainty statement is part of a good engineering explanation."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/missions/mars-2020-perseverance/ingenuity-helicopter/nasa-performs-first-aircraft-accident-investigation-on-another-world/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA · Accident investigation, December 11, 2024"," ",["SPAN",{"class":"sr-only"},"(opens in a new tab)"]]]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button","disabled":""},"Collected: A most-likely explanation"]]]]],["P",{"class":"conclusion-signoff"},"The missions ended. Their stories live on."],["P",{"class":"journey-storage"},"Revisit the spacecraft, review the evidence, or restart the investigation above."]],[[396,694]],[[396,703]]]]],[[412,101]]]],[[412,104]]]],"viewport":{"width":1440,"height":900},"timestamp":316746.12,"wallTime":1794744302270,"collectionTime":24.19999998807907,"resourceOverrides":[],"isMainFrame":true}}
